Search the new environment's PATH for a #! interpreter.
When the interpreter on a script's #! line is in /bin, /usr/bin, /sbin
or /usr/sbin, is not an applet and does not exist, it is looked up in
PATH. mingw_spawn_interpreter() did this with getenv("PATH"), but the
shell keeps its variables to itself and passes them to the program
only through envp, so the search used the PATH the shell was started
with. After "PATH=/some/dir:$PATH" in a script, a command run from
/some/dir was found but a script there naming it on its #! line failed
with "not found", and an interpreter left out of the new PATH was
still used.

Take PATH from envp when the caller passes one, which only the shell
does, and from the process environment otherwise.

---
 win32/process.c |   15 ++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/win32/process.c
+++ b/win32/process.c
@@ -179,6 +179,19 @@
 	return find_executable(name, &path);
 }
 
+/* Search the PATH in envp, if given, else our own PATH. */
+static char *
+find_first_executable_envp(const char *name, char *const *envp)
+{
+	const char *path = NULL;
+
+	if (!envp)
+		return find_first_executable(name);
+	for (; *envp && !path; envp++)
+		path = is_prefixed_with_case(*envp, "PATH=");
+	return find_executable(name, &path);
+}
+
 static intptr_t
 spawnveq(int mode, const char *path, char *const *argv, char *const *env)
 {
@@ -368,7 +381,7 @@
 
 	path = file_is_win32_exe(interp.path);
 	if (!path && is_unix_path)
-		path = find_first_executable(interp.name);
+		path = find_first_executable_envp(interp.name, envp);
 
 	if (path) {
 		new_argv[0] = path;
